 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Climate Week in New York City used to be about reducing emissions. Now it's about increasing power to data centers.

 Axios Pro reporter Alan Neuhauser has spent the past few days on the ground with execs and investors, in a futile search for actual climate talk. What he's heard a lot about, instead, is bringing as much 24/7 electricity online as fast as possible. If that's via nuclear or renewables, great. If it's via fossil fuels, so be it. Why it matters: Climate change remains a critical global issue. But it's being subordinated to AI demands, like so much else.

 What they're saying: "Climate Week should be rebranded to Get Energy Faster Week," Tesla co-founder and Redwood Materials CEO JB Straubel said Tuesday during an onstage conversation at Axios House.

 Zoom in: The power push is eviscerating due diligence.

 The defining metric is "speed-to-power," or how quickly a new energy source can begin supplying a project or grid. Not climate impact or even long-term viability. "I have never felt this much market pull for our energy companies," one VC told Alan. "Customers are so hungry for any kind of energy source." By the numbers: Projected new natural gas-fired generation in the U.S. alone has doubled to 155 GW by 2035.

 ExxonMobil, citing the buildout, just increased its global emissions forecast by 10%. Between the lines: Climate-focused VCs are still backing zero-emissions technologies, but emissions levels are largely irrelevant for PE and infrastructure firms writing the biggest checks.

 The bottom line: The race to decarbonize has been supplanted by the race to bring power online.

 
 
